<!-- Two shapes, one column: with no file the middle is a pressable drop
     zone that fills the window; with one it is a card — a preview beside
     the file's name and size, with one result row per format underneath,
     spanning the content width. The chrome around them (format chips,
     actions, status) never moves, so the window never reflows out from
     under a press.

     There is NO in-window header. The titlebar already says Smoosh; a
     wordmark and app icon said it twice more in a 540pt window, and the
     row also stranded a Reset button above an empty drop zone offering to
     undo nothing. Reset now sits beside Smoosh and takes the same
     treatment — disabled on exactly the guard that makes a press a no-op.
     The 46px that bought went to the preview frame.

     The copy says "drop", not just "click" — `on_drop` (SDK 0.8.2+) makes
     a real window-wide drag-and-drop land in the same load chain a picked
     file does (see `main.zig`'s `onDrop`/`beginLoad`). The zone itself
     stays a click target too: dropping anywhere in the window works, but
     the zone is still the one thing worth pointing at. It is a WASH, not
     an outline: no `border` attribute exists on a panel and nothing in
     the SDK strokes dashes, so the recess is the whole affordance.

     Every direct child of the root column carries a `key`. Without one,
     unkeyed same-kind siblings take POSITIONAL identity, so each `<if>`
     below that starts rendering re-disambiguates everything after it and
     the widget ids all move — confirmed live, where the Smoosh button
     took three different ids across idle -> ready -> done. Keys pin
     identity regardless of which conditionals are currently in the tree,
     which is what `native automate` scripts (and engine-owned widget
     state) rely on. Validate with: native check -->
<!-- `background="background"` is not decoration — it is a REPAINT fix.
     The window ground is otherwise the surface's clear colour, which is
     not a display-list command, so a theme flip changes it while nothing
     damages the bare regions: the runtime's incremental present repaints
     only the widgets whose commands changed and leaves every uncovered
     patch of ground in the OLD scheme. Toggling light/dark showed the top
     of the window in one scheme and the bottom in the other until the
     next content change forced a full repaint. Painting the ground as a
     real fill puts it in the diff, so a token change damages it like
     anything else. -->
<column background="background">
  <!-- The root column takes NO padding of its own so the separator below
       can bleed to both window edges. The body carries the 16, and the
       footer restates it — `padding` is a single uniform number with no
       per-side values anywhere, so a padded root could not have produced
       a full-width rule. -->
  <column key="body" grow="1" gap="12" padding="16">
    <!-- The middle band. Both arms carry `grow="1"` so the window's spare
         height lands here rather than stretching the control rows. -->
    <if test="{hasFile}">
      <column key="file" gap="12" grow="1">
        <row key="summary" gap="12" cross="center">
          <!-- The preview is an ImageIO thumbnail, not the source image —
               registered images are capped at 1 MiB of decoded RGBA
               (512x512), and the 256 KiB host-result cap the pixels ride
               binds tighter still. Drawn at its own honest size: ImageIO
               CAPS at its long edge rather than upscaling to it, so an
               8x8 icon comes back 8x8 and draws 8x8. -->
          <if test="{hasPreview}">
            <!-- A FIXED 144x144 frame with the image centred inside. The
                 image keeps its honest size, so an 8x8 icon draws 8x8
                 rather than a blurry 144x144 — but the card's geometry no
                 longer depends on it, which is what stops a small source
                 from rendering as a lost dot in an empty row. 144, down
                 from 168, is what pays for the result rows below: beside
                 a 168px preview a row carrying a labelled Save overflowed
                 the 420pt minimum by 40px. -->
            <panel key="preview-frame" width="144" height="144" background="surface_subtle" radius="lg">
              <column grow="1" main="center" cross="center">
                <image image="{image_id}" width="{preview_width}" height="{preview_height}" label="Preview of the chosen image" />
              </column>
            </panel>
          </if>
          <column key="details" grow="1" gap="3">
            <text key="name"><span weight="medium">{fileName}</span></text>
            <text key="subtitle" size="sm" foreground="text_muted">{fileSubtitle}</text>
          </column>
        </row>

        <!-- One card per format in the run, BELOW the preview and
             spanning the content width. Per-format rather than a summed
             "combined savings" — no client ever downloads both files, so
             each row reports what would really be served.

             `showAvifRow`/`showWebpRow`, not `hasXResult`: a row is
             RESERVED at full height for the whole `.compressing` phase,
             so on a "Both" run the slower format fills its own row in
             place instead of inserting above the faster one and punting
             it down a frame later. While a reserved row waits, its size
             is an em dash and its badge + Save are absent (the badge
             arms already gate on `hasXResult`; Save gets its own `<if>`).
             At settle a format that FAILED collapses its row — one
             reflow, off the common path — so "a failed format shows no
             row; the status bar names it" still holds.

             Each row carries its own Save — the file already exists on
             disk next to the source, so it means "copy THIS one file
             elsewhere", not a global action.

             Three registers per row, which is why it is three widgets and
             not one line of text: the format name in ink, the size muted,
             the savings figure in a lilac badge weight-graded by
             `savingsGate` (quiet / outline / solid). `<span>` carries
             weight and scale but NOT `foreground`, so a two-tone line
             cannot be one `<text>`.

             The leading `<spacer>` is the row's left inset, and the
             height is stated rather than padded: `padding` is uniform, so
             the 10pt left inset the design draws would be 10 top and
             bottom too, and a card built around a 28pt `size="sm"`
             control would come out 48. `height="34"` is the design's
             figure directly, with `cross="center"` centring the control
             in it. -->
        <column key="results" gap="6">
          <if test="{showAvifRow}">
            <row key="avif-result" background="surface_subtle" radius="md" height="34" gap="8" cross="center">
              <spacer width="2" />
              <text size="sm" min-width="38"><span weight="medium">AVIF</span></text>
              <text size="sm" foreground="text_muted">{avifSize}</text>
              <spacer grow="1" />
              <!-- The savings figure, weighted by how much this format
                   saved (`savingsGate` in main.zig): borderless muted
                   text under 30%, the stock outline from 30–70%, a solid
                   lilac fill at 70%+. Three `<if>` arms, not one styled
                   badge, because a `<badge>`'s colour channels take
                   token NAMES and no binding — and the SDK forces the
                   shape of each arm:
                     · no `success` badge variant exists, and the fill
                       ignores `background=` — only `variant="primary"`
                       paints a fill, and it reads it from the `accent`
                       style channel, so the win chip is `primary` with
                       `accent`/`accent-foreground` aimed at the
                       `success` pair;
                     · only `outline` wears a border (there is no
                       stroke-width attribute), so `keep` must be
                       `outline` and the other two are borderless;
                     · `ghost` is the borderless, fill-less arm for
                       `quiet`.
                   Each arm states `radius="sm"` (6) — without it a badge
                   falls back to a height/2 full pill on its fixed 20pt
                   frame; 6pt is the 30%-of-height corner that matches the
                   result row. The `keep` arm also states
                   `border-color="success"`, because `variant="outline"`
                   pulls its ring from `tokens.colors.border` (grey) and
                   `foreground` only colours the text. Keyed per arm like
                   the status line's three marks. -->
              <if test="{avifSavingsQuiet}">
                <badge key="avif-badge-quiet" variant="ghost" radius="sm" foreground="text_muted">{avifSavings}</badge>
              </if>
              <if test="{avifSavingsKeep}">
                <badge key="avif-badge-keep" variant="outline" radius="sm" foreground="success" border-color="success">{avifSavings}</badge>
              </if>
              <if test="{avifSavingsWin}">
                <badge key="avif-badge-win" variant="primary" radius="sm" accent="success" accent-foreground="success_text">{avifSavings}</badge>
              </if>
              <!-- `icon="download"`, NOT `icon="save"`: the registry's
                   `save` is a floppy disk — three paths with an inner
                   label plate that collapses into mush at 14px. Its own
                   `<if>` so a reserved-but-pending row shows no Save. -->
              <if test="{hasAvifResult}">
                <!-- The same hover treatment the footer's "Show in
                     Finder" wears, and here it costs nothing structural:
                     `foreground` is a generic style attribute, so a stock
                     `<button>` keeps its icon, its disabled state and its
                     button role while only the INK swaps.

                     `background="surface_subtle"` is what removes every
                     fill, and it has to be the background rather than
                     `quiet-hover`: that knob silences the HOVER wash only
                     (by design — "press feedback, the focus ring, cursor
                     intent and hit testing keep their own channels"), so
                     a press still painted `surface_pressed` under the
                     label. An authored background outranks the whole
                     ladder instead — `buttonFillColor`'s ghost arm ends in
                     `widgetBackgroundColor`, which returns
                     `style.background orelse fallback` before any state is
                     consulted. Aimed at the ROW'S OWN token, so every
                     state paints the colour already behind it, and the two
                     move together if the row is ever recoloured.

                     BOTH ARMS CARRY THE SAME KEY and both hover handlers.
                     Key is widget identity, so this is one button
                     changing ink, not two swapping places — with separate
                     keys the id under the pointer changes at the swap and
                     the runtime's enter/leave pairing strobes the label.
                     See the footer control for the full account. -->
                <if test="{avif_save_hovered}">
                  <button key="avif-save" size="sm" variant="ghost" icon="download" foreground="text"
                          background="surface_subtle" on-press="save_avif_as"
                          on-hover-enter="avif_save_hover_on" on-hover-leave="avif_save_hover_off"
                          disabled="{isSaving}" label="Save AVIF as…">Save</button>
                </if>
                <if test="{not avif_save_hovered}">
                  <button key="avif-save" size="sm" variant="ghost" icon="download" foreground="text_muted"
                          background="surface_subtle" on-press="save_avif_as"
                          on-hover-enter="avif_save_hover_on" on-hover-leave="avif_save_hover_off"
                          disabled="{isSaving}" label="Save AVIF as…">Save</button>
                </if>
              </if>
            </row>
          </if>
          <if test="{showWebpRow}">
            <row key="webp-result" background="surface_subtle" radius="md" height="34" gap="8" cross="center">
              <spacer width="2" />
              <text size="sm" min-width="38"><span weight="medium">WebP</span></text>
              <text size="sm" foreground="text_muted">{webpSize}</text>
              <spacer grow="1" />
              <if test="{webpSavingsQuiet}">
                <badge key="webp-badge-quiet" variant="ghost" radius="sm" foreground="text_muted">{webpSavings}</badge>
              </if>
              <if test="{webpSavingsKeep}">
                <badge key="webp-badge-keep" variant="outline" radius="sm" foreground="success" border-color="success">{webpSavings}</badge>
              </if>
              <if test="{webpSavingsWin}">
                <badge key="webp-badge-win" variant="primary" radius="sm" accent="success" accent-foreground="success_text">{webpSavings}</badge>
              </if>
              <if test="{hasWebpResult}">
                <if test="{webp_save_hovered}">
                  <button key="webp-save" size="sm" variant="ghost" icon="download" foreground="text"
                          background="surface_subtle" on-press="save_webp_as"
                          on-hover-enter="webp_save_hover_on" on-hover-leave="webp_save_hover_off"
                          disabled="{isSaving}" label="Save WebP as…">Save</button>
                </if>
                <if test="{not webp_save_hovered}">
                  <button key="webp-save" size="sm" variant="ghost" icon="download" foreground="text_muted"
                          background="surface_subtle" on-press="save_webp_as"
                          on-hover-enter="webp_save_hover_on" on-hover-leave="webp_save_hover_off"
                          disabled="{isSaving}" label="Save WebP as…">Save</button>
                </if>
              </if>
            </row>
          </if>
        </column>
      </column>
    </if>
    <else>
      <panel key="dropzone" grow="1" padding="16" background="surface_subtle" radius="xl"
             on-press="pick_file" label="Drop an image, or click to choose one to compress">
        <column gap="6" grow="1" main="center" cross="center">
          <icon name="folder-open" width="28" height="28" foreground="text_muted" />
          <text text-alignment="center">Drop an image here — or click to choose</text>
          <text size="sm" foreground="text_muted" text-alignment="center">JPEG, PNG, HEIC, WebP, TIFF or GIF, up to 100 MB</text>
        </column>
      </panel>
    </else>

    <!-- ONE control row: the format choice on the left, the two actions
         on the right, everything on the same baseline. The canvas draws
         these as two stacked rows; folded into one they read as a single
         "set it, then run it" line and give the tallest state back the
         vertical room that a second row and its 12pt gap cost.

         Everything in it is `size="sm"`, which is what makes the row one
         height: the SDK's control rungs are sm 32 / default 40, so a
         default-size Smoosh beside a sm Reset differs by 8px and cannot
         be aligned. Hierarchy comes from the fill and the weight.

         The segmented control: `<toggle-group>` paints NOTHING at all (an
         explicit no-op arm in the render switch), so the track is the
         wrapping `<row>` and the thumb is the ghost toggle-button's own
         selected state — transparent at rest, `surface_subtle` when
         selected. That is why `surface_pressed` is the darker step in
         both schemes (see `palette` in main.zig): the track has to sit
         UNDER the thumb. The label stays "Format" rather than a
         tracked-out FORMAT — there is no letter-spacing to track with.

         Each action disables on exactly the guard its own `update` arm
         enforces, so a press that would be a silent no-op is shown as
         unavailable: Smoosh on `canSmoosh`, Reset on `hasFile`. Choosing
         a file lives on the drop zone above, and saving a copy lives on
         each result row. -->
    <row key="actions" gap="8" cross="center">
      <text size="sm" foreground="text_muted">Format</text>
      <row background="surface_pressed" radius="md" padding="1">
        <toggle-group gap="2" label="Format">
          <for each="formats" as="f">
            <toggle-button size="sm" variant="ghost" selected="{f.value == format}" on-toggle="set_format:{f.value}">{f.label}</toggle-button>
          </for>
        </toggle-group>
      </row>
      <spacer grow="1" />
      <!-- Both actions are stated at 30 — the segmented track's height
           (a 28pt control plus its 1pt of track on each side), so the
           whole row is one silhouette rather than a 30pt pill beside two
           28pt ones. The buttons cannot reach it through the control
           rungs: sm IS 28, and the track is by construction the segment
           height plus its own padding, so it is always taller than the
           control inside it.

           Smoosh also carries a `min-width`. The house register holds one
           10pt horizontal inset across every rung, so a button hugs its
           label; the design gives the primary action 18 a side, and the
           extra width is most of what makes it read as the thing to
           press. `min-width` buys it on THIS button alone — raising
           `button_inset_sm` would widen Reset, both Save buttons and
           every segment with it, and the row has no room to spare at the
           420pt floor. -->
      <button size="sm" height="30" variant="ghost" on-press="reset" disabled="{not hasFile}">Reset</button>
      <button size="sm" height="30" min-width="88" variant="primary" on-press="smoosh" disabled="{not canSmoosh}">Smoosh</button>
    </row>
  </column>

  <!-- A full-bleed hairline and about 10px, which is the Mac answer: it
       divides machine chatter from the controls without introducing a
       second surface, and it survives the empty state — a rule with
       nothing under it still reads as a status area, where a bare gap
       reads as nothing at all. A filled band was the alternative and puts
       a second cream surface directly under the cream drop zone, which in
       a 400pt window is a permanent 34px carrying nothing. -->
  <separator key="footer-rule" />

  <row key="status" gap="8" cross="center" padding="16">
    <!-- One slot, three faces. A spinner while work is in flight, an
         alert on failure, a lilac dot on success — all beside the bar
         rather than recolouring it, because two `<status-bar>`s behind an
         if/else would give the status line two different widget ids and
         automation scripts address it as one. The failure mark is
         `alert` (a circle with a bang), not a triangle. -->
    <if test="{isBusy}">
      <spinner key="busy" width="14" height="14" />
    </if>
    <if test="{isFailed}">
      <icon key="failed" name="alert" width="14" height="14" foreground="destructive" label="Failed" />
    </if>
    <!-- A filled 7px dot, which no icon in the registry is: `circle-dot`
         draws a ring around one. A panel is the dot — `radius` clamps to
         half the box, so `sm` (6) still rounds a 7px square to a circle. -->
    <if test="{isDone}">
      <panel key="done-dot" width="7" height="7" background="success" radius="sm" label="Done" />
    </if>
    <!-- A plain `<text>`, NOT a `<status-bar>`. The widget paints a band
         whether or not you want one: an unconditional `surface` fill over
         its whole frame, its own top hairline (a second rule directly
         under the separator above), and a fixed 14pt text inset that
         `padding` cannot clear — `padding="0"` falls back to the default.
         That band IS the filled-footer treatment this design rejected,
         and it also broke the left edge, sitting the status text 14pt in
         from the content above it.

         The design's constraint was never the KIND: one status widget,
         one id, one colour, so the mark beside it carries the tone and
         automation addresses the line as one thing. A `<text>` keeps
         every part of that. -->
    <text key="status-line" size="sm" foreground="text_muted">{statusLine}</text>
    <!-- "Show in Finder": the auto-write's only affordance. Smoosh writes
         beside the source without asking, and until this button existed
         nothing in the window ever said where that was.

         A `ghost` button, which the widget layer draws as content only —
         no fill, no stroke — so it reads as the link it is rather than as
         a third command competing with Reset and Smoosh. There is no
         `link` variant in `WidgetVariant`; `ghost` is the text-button
         face.

         No height of its own, matching `scheme-toggle` beside it: that
         button already sets this row's height, and giving this one the
         `height="30"` the action row uses would grow the footer and eat
         into the layout floor `tests.zig` pins.

         Conditional, and keyed — same shape as `done-dot` above. This is
         one control that exists in one state, not two alternative widgets
         for one control, so it has one stable id. -->
    <if test="{canReveal}">
      <!-- NOT a `<button>`, and the only hand-assembled control in the
           app. The design is a link whose LABEL brightens under the
           pointer, and no stock control can do that: a ghost button's ink
           is one value in every state (`buttonTextColorForWidget`'s
           `.ghost` arm reads no hover or pressed channel),
           `ControlVisualTokens` has no `hover_foreground`, and markup's
           `foreground` takes a literal token name and refuses a binding —
           "dynamic styling stays in Zig". A button's hover WASH is all it
           would offer, and a wash is what this design rejected.

           So the hover becomes model state (`on-hover-enter` /
           `on-hover-leave` are real markup events, and binding them makes
           an element hover-hittable without claiming presses) and the ink
           becomes two `<if>` arms — the same shape the savings badge
           above uses, for the same reason.

           BOTH ARMS CARRY THE SAME KEY, and that is the load-bearing
           detail. Key is widget identity, so the two arms are one widget
           whose ink changes rather than two widgets swapping places.
           Different keys would make the id under the pointer change at
           the moment of the swap, and the runtime pairs every enter with
           a leave it captured BEFORE dispatching (`ui_app.zig`: "an enter
           whose own handler unmounts the element still has its leave to
           deliver") — so the enter that turns hover ON would be followed
           by the vanished arm's leave turning it straight off again, and
           the label would strobe under a resting pointer.

           A `<text>` and not a container, because a container would bring
           chrome this does not want: a pressable `<row>` paints
           `listItemFillColor`'s hover wash and refuses `quiet-hover`
           (`hit_target = false` in the element registry), and a `<panel>`
           takes `quiet-hover` but draws its own fill and hairline. Text
           paints neither. -->
      <if test="{reveal_hovered}">
        <text key="reveal" size="sm" foreground="text" label="Show in Finder"
              on-press="show_in_finder"
              on-hover-enter="reveal_hover_on" on-hover-leave="reveal_hover_off">Show in Finder</text>
      </if>
      <if test="{not reveal_hovered}">
        <text key="reveal" size="sm" foreground="text_muted" label="Show in Finder"
              on-press="show_in_finder"
              on-hover-enter="reveal_hover_on" on-hover-leave="reveal_hover_off">Show in Finder</text>
      </if>
    </if>
    <!-- Takes the slack the status text used to hold, so the button sits
         against the end of the message ("Done. Show in Finder") instead
         of at the far right of the row. -->
    <spacer grow="1" />
    <!-- The appearance toggle. It offers the OTHER scheme — `moon` while
         light, `sun` while dark — so the glyph names what a press
         produces. Icon-only, so `label` is its accessible name, and both
         come from the model rather than an `<if>`: two conditional
         buttons would be two widget ids for one control. -->
    <button key="scheme-toggle" size="sm" variant="ghost" icon="{schemeIcon}"
            on-press="toggle_color_scheme" label="{schemeToggleLabel}" />
  </row>
</column>
